        <div id="what-is-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Hydrolyzed Whey Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey protein is a mix of long protein chains, mainly beta-lactoglobulin and alpha-lactalbumin, plus smaller fractions. Your gut digests those chains by snipping the bonds between amino acids until it is left with free amino acids and short peptides it can absorb. Hydrolysis does some of that snipping in the factory. Manufacturers treat whey with enzymes, similar in principle to the proteases in your own digestive tract, and stop the reaction once the protein has been broken into a mix of shorter peptides.
          </p>

          <p>
            The result is still whey. It has the same amino acid profile, the same leucine content per gram of protein and the same overall protein quality as the whey it started from, because hydrolysis rearranges the package rather than changing the contents. What changes is the size of the pieces. A hydrolysate contains more small peptides and free amino acids, which is why it is marketed as pre-digested, faster absorbing and gentler on the stomach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrolysis can be applied to whey concentrate or whey isolate, but in sports nutrition the base is almost always isolate. That matters because isolate has already been filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat, so a hydrolyzed whey isolate is low in lactose for the same reason any isolate is, not because hydrolysis removes lactose. Hydrolysis acts on protein, not on milk sugar.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Hydrolysis changes the size of the pieces, not the protein.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A hydrolyzed whey has the same amino acids and leucine as the whey it came from. You pay for smaller peptides and a slightly faster rise in blood amino acids, not for more muscle-building protein per gram.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="degree" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Degree of Hydrolysis: Why Not All Hydrolyzed Whey Is Equal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Degree of hydrolysis, often shortened to DH, is the share of the protein's peptide bonds that have been broken. A whey with a low DH has been nicked here and there and still contains plenty of larger fragments. A whey with a high DH has been broken down extensively into small peptides and free amino acids. Food scientists measure it directly, and it is the single most useful number for comparing hydrolysates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unfortunately, supplement brands almost never print it. Sports hydrolysates are typically described in vague terms such as partially hydrolyzed or fully hydrolyzed, and the label's ingredient list is the only clue you get. A powder that lists hydrolyzed whey protein isolate as its first or only protein is likely to contain a lot of hydrolysate. A powder that lists whey peptides as the third item in a blend may contain very little, and you have no way to tell how little.
          </p>

          <p>
            DH has practical consequences. The higher it goes, the more bitter the powder tastes, the thinner it mixes and the more it tends to cost to make palatable. That is why most mainstream hydrolyzed wheys sit at the low-to-moderate end: enough hydrolysis to claim the benefit, not so much that the shake becomes hard to drink. Extensively hydrolyzed proteins, the kind used in medical and infant formulas, are a different product class with a very different taste and price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="absorption" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does Hydrolyzed Whey Absorb Faster? What the Research Shows
          </h2>

          <p>
            Partly, yes. Studies that compare hydrolyzed and intact proteins generally find that hydrolysates produce a quicker rise in blood amino acids after drinking. A 2009 study by Koopman and colleagues in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, for example, found that hydrolyzed casein was digested and absorbed faster than intact casein in older men. Whey is already a fast protein, though, so the gap between hydrolyzed and intact whey is smaller than the gap between whey and casein.
          </p>

          <p>
            A widely quoted 2009 study by Tang and colleagues in the Journal of Applied Physiology found that whey hydrolysate stimulated more muscle protein synthesis than micellar casein and somewhat more than soy, at rest and after exercise. That study is often cited as proof that hydrolyzed whey is superior, but it did not include intact whey isolate. It shows that a fast whey beats a slow casein in the hours after a workout, which is not the same claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            The longer-term evidence is the part that should guide your money. In an eight-week trial published in the Journal of the American College of Nutrition in 2017, Lockwood and colleagues gave resistance-trained college-aged men whey concentrate, whey isolate or whey hydrolysate alongside a training program. All groups improved, and the hydrolyzed whey did not produce meaningfully better changes in body composition or strength than the other wheys. That matches the broader picture: total daily protein and consistent training drive results, and the form of whey is a minor detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where faster digestion may plausibly help is in situations where you have a short window between hard sessions, such as two-a-day training or tournament days, or when a heavy shake would sit badly before the next bout. Even there, the evidence is mechanistic rather than proven in outcomes. Our guide on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> covers timing in more detail, and the honest summary is that hitting your daily target matters far more than the minute you drink it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-isolate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hydrolyzed Whey vs Whey Isolate: Which Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people, buy isolate. A good whey isolate already delivers around 25 g of protein in roughly 100 to 120 calories, with very little lactose and fat, and it costs less than a hydrolysate with a comparable label. If you are moving up from a concentrate because of bloating, the isolate step is the one that usually solves it. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> comparison explains why lactose is the usual culprit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrolyzed whey is worth trying in three situations. First, if an isolate still leaves you uncomfortable and you have ruled out sweeteners, gums and portion size as the cause, some people report that a hydrolysate sits more easily. Second, if you train twice a day and want the lightest possible protein between sessions. Third, if the hydrolyzed product you are looking at happens to be priced like an isolate, which is close to true for ISO100 and R1, because both blend hydrolysate with isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrolyzed whey is not worth paying extra for if your goal is general muscle gain or weight loss, if you are buying your first protein powder, or if you mainly drink shakes with meals or before bed. In all those cases, the speed advantage is irrelevant and the bitterness and cost are real downsides. A casein or blend may even suit evening use better, as our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide explains.
          </p>

