            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is hydrolyzed whey better than whey isolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not for most goals. Hydrolyzed whey digests slightly faster, but an eight-week trial by Lockwood and colleagues found no meaningful advantage in body composition or strength over isolate or concentrate. It is worth trying if isolate still bothers your stomach or you train twice a day; otherwise isolate gives the same protein for less money.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is hydrolyzed whey easier to digest?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can be for some people, because the protein arrives partly broken down. But much of the comfort people notice comes from the low lactose of the isolate base rather than the hydrolysis. If ordinary whey causes bloating, try a plain isolate first, and check for sugar alcohols or gums that may be the real cause.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does hydrolyzed whey absorb faster?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Generally, yes. Hydrolysates tend to raise blood amino acids a little faster than intact proteins. Whey is already fast, so the difference versus whey isolate is modest, and the research has not shown that it leads to more muscle over time. Hitting your daily protein total matters much more than absorption speed.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can people with a milk allergy use hydrolyzed whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Sports hydrolyzed whey is only partly broken down and still contains milk proteins that can trigger an allergic reaction. Extensively hydrolyzed formulas used for infants with cow's milk protein allergy are a different, medically supervised product. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid whey and speak to a doctor or allergist.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why does hydrolyzed whey taste bitter?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Breaking whey into smaller peptides exposes hydrophobic amino acids, and many of those short peptides taste bitter. The more extensive the hydrolysis, the stronger the effect. Flavored powders mask it well, chocolate and coffee best of all, while unflavored hydrolysates are noticeably harsher than unflavored isolates.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is hydrolyzed whey worth the money?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only for a narrow group: people whose stomachs still react to isolate, athletes training twice a day, or anyone who finds a hydrolyzed blend priced like an isolate. For general muscle gain, weight loss or a first protein powder, a good isolate or even a concentrate gives the same results for less.
